  _globals['_TRIGGERWEBHOOKWRAPPERREQUEST'].fields_by_name['request_body']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERWEBHOOKWRAPPERREQUEST'].fields_by_name['request_body']._serialized_options = b'\222AC2ARequest body payload. Only used for POST, PUT, and PATCH methods.'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE']._serialized_options = b'\222AR\n\010Triggers\022FSubscribe an agent to events from a connected third-party application.'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['ListAvailableTriggers']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['ListAvailableTriggers']._serialized_options = b'\222A\254\003\n\010Triggers\022\027List available triggers\032\276\002Retrieves every trigger the connection\'s provider publishes, each marked available or not for this specific connection. A trigger that cannot run here is still returned, carrying both the reason it was refused and the remedy \342\200\224 omitting it would leave a customer unable to work out why a documented option is missing.JF\n\003200\022?\n=Successfully retrieved the triggers this connection can offer\202\265\030\033\n\027connected_accounts:read\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002.\022,/api/v1/connections/{connection_id}/triggers'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['ListEligibleAccounts']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['ListEligibleAccounts']._serialized_options = b'\222A\363\004\n\010Triggers\022$List accounts eligible for a trigger\032\374\003Retrieves the connection\'s accounts, each marked eligible or not for this trigger. An ineligible account is returned with the reason and remedy rather than filtered out, so a customer can see why the account they expected to pick is refused. The list is paginated and searchable by identifier: total_size counts this connection\'s own accounts matching the query \342\200\224 never accounts belonging to another connector, which cannot receive this trigger under any configuration and play no part in the count at all.JB\n\003200\022;\n9Successfully retrieved the accounts and their eligibility\202\265\030\033\n\027connected_accounts:read\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002O\022M/api/v1/connections/{connection_id}/triggers/{trigger_name}/eligible-accounts'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['ListTriggerSubscriptions']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['ListTriggerSubscriptions']._serialized_options = b'\222A\200\003\n\010Triggers\022\032List trigger subscriptions\032\217\002Retrieves the trigger subscriptions on a connection, including their status and, for polled triggers, both the requested and the effective interval. Both intervals are returned because a subscription showing only its effective rate is indistinguishable from a broken one.JF\n\003200\022?\n=Successfully retrieved the connection\'s trigger subscriptions\202\265\030\033\n\027connected_accounts:read\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002;\0229/api/v1/connections/{connection_id}/trigger-subscriptions'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['CreateTriggerSubscription']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['CreateTriggerSubscription']._serialized_options = b'\222A\202\004\n\010Triggers\022\026Subscribe to a trigger\032\245\003Subscribes a connected account to a trigger. Every refusal names its remedy: a missing scope names the scope, an interval below the trigger\'s floor names the minimum and what was asked for, and an account authorized under a different app says so. An interval below the floor is refused rather than rounded up, because a customer who asked for 30 seconds and was silently given 300 will build on the number they asked for.J6\n\003200\022/\n-Successfully created the trigger subscription\202\265\030\034\n\030connected_accounts:write\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002>\"9/api/v1/connections/{connection_id}/trigger-subscriptions:\001*'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['BatchCreateTriggerSubscriptions']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['BatchCreateTriggerSubscriptions']._serialized_options = b'\222A\220\016\n\010Triggers\022\'Subscribe several accounts to a trigger\032\236\007Subscribes many connected accounts to one trigger with one filter, and reports what happened to each account separately. Use this instead of repeating the single-account call when onboarding a connection that already has many accounts. The call is best-effort rather than all-or-nothing: a refused account does not undo one that succeeded, because refusals here are ordinary rather than exceptional \342\200\224 an account may hold a revoked credential, lack a scope, or already carry this exact filter. Each result names its own outcome, and a refusal names its remedy. Retrying the whole request is safe: an account subscribed by an earlier attempt comes back as already subscribed rather than as an error. A connection-scoped trigger is subscribed on the connection itself rather than per account: send an empty connected_account_ids for it, and the one subscription comes back as a single result with an empty connected_account_id.J\201\001\n\003200\022z\nxRequest processed; every account has its own outcome in results, and created_count says how many subscriptions were madeJ\212\004\n\003400\022\202\004\n\377\003The request was refused as a whole \342\200\224 the filter is invalid for this trigger, the requested interval is below the trigger\'s minimum, an account id is malformed, or connected_account_ids is empty for a trigger that is subscribed per connected account. No subscription was created. A well-formed id naming an account that does not exist is NOT this case: it comes back as a per-account NOT_FOUND result with a 200, so one bad id in a batch of a hundred does not discard the ninety-nine that would have succeeded.J\250\001\n\003404\022\240\001\n\235\001The trigger does not exist on this connection\'s provider. Refused as a whole, since a trigger nothing can subscribe to has no per-account outcomes to report.\202\265\030\034\n\030connected_accounts:write\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002J\"E/api/v1/connections/{connection_id}/trigger-subscriptions:batchCreate:\001*'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['DeleteTriggerSubscription']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['DeleteTriggerSubscription']._serialized_options = b'\222A\310\001\n\010Triggers\022\035Delete a trigger subscription\032eRemoves a trigger subscription. Delivery stops immediately; events already handed off are unaffected.J6\n\003200\022/\n-Successfully deleted the trigger subscription\202\265\030\034\n\030connected_accounts:write\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002M*K/api/v1/connections/{connection_id}/trigger-subscriptions/{subscription_id}'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['BatchDeleteTriggerSubscriptions']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['BatchDeleteTriggerSubscriptions']._serialized_options = b'\222A\247\032\n\010Triggers\022$Delete several trigger subscriptions\032\343\nDeletes many trigger subscriptions on one connection, and reports what happened to each one separately. Use this to unsubscribe a selection from the subscription list, or every subscription a set of connected accounts holds to one trigger, instead of repeating DeleteTriggerSubscription. Name the subscriptions in exactly one of two ways: by `subscription_ids`, or by `trigger_name` together with `connected_account_ids`, which deletes every subscription those accounts hold to that trigger on this connection, whatever its filter. `trigger_name` alone is refused, so no request can delete every subscription to a trigger. The call is best-effort rather than all-or-nothing: a subscription that could not be deleted does not bring back one that was. Each result names its own outcome: DELETED, NOT_FOUND, FAILED, or NOT_ATTEMPTED when the request ran out of time before reaching it. Retrying the whole request is safe: a subscription deleted by an earlier attempt comes back as NOT_FOUND, so treat DELETED and NOT_FOUND alike as gone. This is where the batch differs from DeleteTriggerSubscription, which answers 404 for a missing subscription because that subscription is its whole answer; in a batch it is one result among many. Delivery stops for each deleted subscription exactly as with DeleteTriggerSubscription: an event already handed off for delivery is still delivered.J\212\002\n\003200\022\202\002\n\274\001Request processed; every subscription ID, or every subscription found for the named accounts, has its own result in results, and deleted_count says how many subscriptions this call deleted\022A\n?\032=.scalekit.v1.triggers.BatchDeleteTriggerSubscriptionsResponseJ\307\010\n\003400\022\277\010\n\274\010The request was refused as a whole and nothing was deleted: both selectors or neither were sent, `trigger_name` was sent without `connected_account_ids`, or `connected_account_ids` without `trigger_name` (`choose one selector: subscription_ids, or trigger_name with connected_account_ids`); a list holds more than 100 IDs or repeats one, including two spellings of the same ID (`duplicate subscription_id at index N: \342\200\246`); an ID is malformed \342\200\224 `connection_id` is not a connection ID, an entry of `subscription_ids` is not a trigger subscription ID (`trgs_`), or an entry of `connected_account_ids` is not a connected account ID (`ca_`); or the named accounts hold more than 1000 subscriptions to the trigger (`the selected accounts hold more than 1000 subscriptions to this trigger; send fewer connected_account_ids per request`). A well-formed ID naming a subscription that does not exist, was already deleted, or belongs to another connection or environment is NOT this case: it comes back as a NOT_FOUND result with a 200, so one stale ID does not discard the rest of the batch.J\346\001\n\003404\022\336\001\n\333\001AgentKit Triggers is not enabled for this workspace, or `connection_id` can never name a connection. Refused as a whole; nothing was deleted. A missing subscription is NOT this case: it is a NOT_FOUND result with a 200.J\232\001\n\003429\022\222\001\n\217\001This environment has sent too many batch delete requests in the last minute. Refused as a whole; nothing was deleted. Retry after a short wait.J\261\001\n\003500\022\251\001\n\246\001The subscriptions that `trigger_name` and `connected_account_ids` name could not be read, so nothing was deleted. Only that selector fails this way; retrying is safe.\202\265\030\034\n\030connected_accounts:write\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002J\"E/api/v1/connections/{connection_id}/trigger-subscriptions:batchDelete:\001*'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['GetTriggerActivity']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['GetTriggerActivity']._serialized_options = b'\222A\374\003\n\010Triggers\022\034Read trigger activity counts\032\231\003Counts of what happened to this environment\'s trigger events over the last day, so the three common causes of \"my trigger didn\'t fire\" can be told apart without contacting support: nothing arriving, events arriving and being filtered out, or events arriving for a provider workspace no connected account holds. Counts only \342\200\224 there is no per-event record to look up, and `explains_individual_events` says so.J6\n\003200\022/\n-Successfully read the trigger activity counts\202\265\030\021\n\rwebhooks:read\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002\032\022\030/api/v1/trigger-activity'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['GetTriggerUsage']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['GetTriggerUsage']._serialized_options = b'\222A\266\034\n\010Triggers\022\032Read metered trigger usage\032\272\021Returns the trigger deliveries metered for this environment over a period, either a preset period ending now or a range of UTC calendar dates. Use it to chart trigger usage, for example the last 24 hours by the hour or the current month by the day. Ask for the period in one of two ways, never both: `period`, or `from` and `to`. With `period=24h` the counts are hourly, in `hours`, for the 24 UTC clock hours ending with the hour in progress, which the response names as `current_hour`; that hour\'s count grows until it ends. With `period=7d` or `period=30d` the counts are daily, in `days`, for the UTC calendar days ending with today\'s UTC date: the week ending today, or the 30 days ending today. Days follow UTC, not the caller\'s time zone. With `from` and `to` the counts are daily, in `days`, for each UTC calendar day from `from` to `to`, both inclusive. Units are deliveries metered by Metronome, the billing system: one unit for each trigger event sent to one receiving endpoint, so an event accepted for delivery to a subscription counts once for every endpoint it is sent to, and a filtered, duplicate or failed handoff, or an event no endpoint receives, counts none. Only hours and days with metered units are returned: one with none is absent rather than listed as zero, so a client fills the gaps itself over the bounds the response returns. The figures trail deliveries by Metronome\'s ingestion delay plus up to a minute of caching, so the latest deliveries can be missing from the current hour\'s and today\'s count for a short while. The readable window is today\'s UTC date and the 30 days before it, and every `period` falls inside it. A `from` and `to` range that extends past the window is clamped to it: a `from` more than 30 days before today\'s UTC date reads from the first day of the window, and a `to` after today\'s UTC date reads through today. A range with no day inside the window is refused, as is a `from` after `to`. The response returns the range actually read as `from` and `to`, which differ from the request when it was clamped, the `period` asked for, the size of each entry as `bucket`, and the server\'s UTC date as `today`, so a client can tell the day still in progress apart from completed ones.J\352\001\n\003200\022\342\001\n\254\001Successfully read the metered usage; includes one entry per hour (`period=24h`, in `hours`) or per UTC day (every other request, in `days`) with metered units, oldest first\0221\n/\032-.scalekit.v1.triggers.GetTriggerUsageResponseJ\257\005\n\003400\022\247\005\n\244\005Invalid request. Refused as a validation error whose details name the rule that failed: `period` is not exactly `24h`, `7d` or `30d`; `period` is sent together with `from` or `to`; `period` is not sent and `from` or `to` is missing, reported as `from is required, as a YYYY-MM-DD UTC date` or `to is required, as a YYYY-MM-DD UTC date`; or a date is not in YYYY-MM-DD form. Refused with the reason as the error message: a date is not a real calendar date, `from` is after `to`, `from` is after today\'s UTC date, or `to` is more than 30 days before today\'s UTC date. A range that only extends past the readable window is clamped, not refused. Checked before Metronome is calledJ\240\002\n\003404\022\230\002\n\225\002Trigger usage is not metered for this environment - its workspace has no Metronome customer, or Metronome is not configured in this region. The error code is `TRIGGER_USAGE_NOT_METERED`. A workspace without AgentKit Triggers enabled also gets a 404, with a different error codeJ\216\001\n\003503\022\206\001\n\203\001Metronome is unavailable - it returned an error or did not answer in time. No partial figures are returned; retry the request later\202\265\030\021\n\rwebhooks:read\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002\027\022\025/api/v1/trigger-usage'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['GetTriggerIngressEndpoint']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['GetTriggerIngressEndpoint']._serialized_options = b'\222A\363\007\n\010Triggers\022#Get a connection\'s ingress endpoint\032\242\004Retrieves the URL a provider posts events to for this connection, together with whether a signing secret has been set and where any rotation has got to. Use this operation when setting a webhook trigger up: the URL goes into the provider\'s console, and the signing secret must be set before the provider will accept it. The signing secret is never returned \342\200\224 only a masked form, enough to tell which secret is stored without disclosing it. Read-only: this operation never creates the endpoint. Create it first with CreateTriggerIngressEndpoint.J\246\001\n\003200\022\236\001\njSuccessfully retrieved the ingress endpoint, its masked signing-secret state, and any rotation in progress\0220\n.\032,.scalekit.v1.triggers.TriggerIngressEndpointJ\363\001\n\003404\022\353\001\n\350\001No webhook URL has been created for this connection yet (error_code TRIGGER_INGRESS_ENDPOINT_NOT_CREATED); call CreateTriggerIngressEndpoint. Also returned when the connection does not exist in this environment (RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND).\202\265\030\033\n\027connected_accounts:read\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\0025\0223/api/v1/connections/{connection_id}/trigger-ingress'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['CreateTriggerIngressEndpoint']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['CreateTriggerIngressEndpoint']._serialized_options = b'\222A\364\t\n\010Triggers\022!Create a connection\'s webhook URL\032\242\005Creates the URL a provider posts this connection\'s events to, and returns it with its signing-secret state. Use this operation when setting a webhook trigger up: the URL goes into the provider\'s console, and must exist before a signing secret can be set with SetTriggerSigningSecret. Only connections whose provider publishes at least one webhook trigger can have one; a provider whose triggers are all polled receives nothing and is refused. Idempotent: when the connection already has a URL, it is returned unchanged and nothing is written, so retrying never replaces a URL already registered with the provider. The signing secret is never returned \342\200\224 only a masked form.J\225\001\n\003200\022\215\001\nYSuccessfully created the webhook URL, or returned the connection\'s existing one unchanged\0220\n.\032,.scalekit.v1.triggers.TriggerIngressEndpointJ\313\002\n\003400\022\303\002\n\300\002The connection\'s provider publishes no webhook trigger, so it has no use for a webhook URL (error_code TRIGGER_INGRESS_NOT_APPLICABLE); or the connector\'s webhook trigger definitions are not available right now and need to be re-synced (error_code TRIGGER_INGRESS_DEFINITIONS_UNAVAILABLE). Nothing is created either way.J:\n\003404\0223\n1The connection does not exist in this environment\202\265\030\034\n\030connected_accounts:write\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\0025\"3/api/v1/connections/{connection_id}/trigger-ingress'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['SetTriggerSigningSecret']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['SetTriggerSigningSecret']._serialized_options = b'\222A\233\014\n\010Triggers\022!Set a connection\'s signing secret\032\342\004Stores the secret the provider signs its events with, taken from the provider\'s own app configuration. Setting a secret where one already exists starts a rotation rather than replacing it: both the old and the new secret are accepted until the rotation is confirmed, so events in flight while the customer updates their provider console are not rejected as forgeries. The response reports the rotation state, including signing_secret_rotation_id; confirm it with ConfirmTriggerSigningSecretRotation, passing that id, once the provider has been updated. The secret is write-only and is never returned or logged.J\235\001\n\003200\022\225\001\naSuccessfully stored the signing secret; the response states whether a rotation is now in progress\0220\n.\032,.scalekit.v1.triggers.TriggerIngressEndpointJ\275\002\n\003400\022\265\002\n\262\002The signing secret is empty or longer than the accepted maximum; or the connection\'s provider sends no signature of its own, so its secret is generated by Scalekit rather than set (error_code TRIGGER_SIGNING_SECRET_GENERATED_ONLY) - call GenerateTriggerSigningSecret instead. Nothing is written either way.J\371\001\n\003404\022\361\001\n\356\001No webhook URL has been created for this connection yet (error_code TRIGGER_INGRESS_ENDPOINT_NOT_CREATED); call CreateTriggerIngressEndpoint first. Also returned when the connection does not exist in this environment (RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND).J\252\001\n\003409\022\242\001\n\237\001A rotation is already in progress - confirm the current one before starting another, or the secret the customer has just installed would be retired unconfirmed\202\265\030\034\n\030connected_accounts:write\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002G\032B/api/v1/connections/{connection_id}/trigger-ingress/signing-secret:\001*'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['ConfirmTriggerSigningSecretRotation']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['ConfirmTriggerSigningSecretRotation']._serialized_options = b'\222A\317\014\n\010Triggers\022!Confirm a signing-secret rotation\032\352\006Retires the previous signing secret, ending a rotation. Call this once the provider\'s console holds the new secret. Retirement is deliberately explicit rather than automatic: a request arriving under the new secret proves the new secret works, but not that the customer has finished, and a provider that signs some traffic with each would have the old secret withdrawn mid-changeover. Until this is called the previous secret stays accepted, bounded by its expiry so an abandoned rotation cannot leave it valid indefinitely. Pass the signing_secret_rotation_id from the SetTriggerSigningSecret or GetTriggerIngressEndpoint response to confirm that specific rotation. Idempotent: calling this when no rotation is in progress returns the endpoint\'s current state rather than an error, since the caller\'s intent \342\200\224 only the current secret should be accepted \342\200\224 already holds.J\307\001\n\003200\022\277\001\n\212\001Successfully retired the previous signing secret, or confirmed there was none in progress; only the current secret is accepted from now on\0220\n.\032,.scalekit.v1.triggers.TriggerIngressEndpointJ\354\001\n\003400\022\344\001\n\341\001signing_secret_rotation_id does not match the endpoint\'s current rotation - a newer rotation has already started since the caller last read the endpoint\'s state; fetch it again with GetTriggerIngressEndpoint before confirmingJ\371\001\n\003404\022\361\001\n\356\001No webhook URL has been created for this connection yet (error_code TRIGGER_INGRESS_ENDPOINT_NOT_CREATED); call CreateTriggerIngressEndpoint first. Also returned when the connection does not exist in this environment (RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND).\202\265\030\034\n\030connected_accounts:write\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002O\"J/api/v1/connections/{connection_id}/trigger-ingress/signing-secret/confirm:\001*'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['GenerateTriggerSigningSecret']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['GenerateTriggerSigningSecret']._serialized_options = b'\222A\223\021\n\010Triggers\022&Generate a connection\'s signing secret\032\376\007Generates a random signing secret for this connection\'s webhook URL, stores it, and returns it in plain text together with the URL that carries it. Use this operation for a provider that sends no signature of its own (provides_bearer_secret is true on its triggers): the provider presents the secret back on every event, in an `Authorization: Bearer <secret>` header, the `X-Scalekit-Webhook-Secret` header, or the final path segment of url_with_secret, and an event without it is rejected. The first call on an endpoint with no secret sets one. A call where a secret already exists starts a rotation rather than replacing it, exactly as SetTriggerSigningSecret does: both secrets are accepted until the rotation is confirmed with ConfirmTriggerSigningSecretRotation. The plaintext secret appears only in this response and is never returned again or logged; generate another if it is lost. A provider that signs its own events takes its secret from its console through SetTriggerSigningSecret instead, and is refused here.J\233\002\n\003200\022\223\002\n\320\001Successfully generated and stored the signing secret; the response carries it in plain text, the webhook URL with the secret appended, and the endpoint\'s state, including whether a rotation is now in progress\022>\n<\032:.scalekit.v1.triggers.GenerateTriggerSigningSecretResponseJ\353\002\n\003400\022\343\002\n\340\002The connection\'s provider signs its own events, so its secret is copied from the provider\'s console and set with SetTriggerSigningSecret rather than generated (error_code TRIGGER_SIGNING_SECRET_NOT_GENERATABLE); nothing is written. Also returned when connection_id is empty, longer than 64 characters, or not a connection identifier (INVALID_ARGUMENT).J\371\001\n\003404\022\361\001\n\356\001No webhook URL has been created for this connection yet (error_code TRIGGER_INGRESS_ENDPOINT_NOT_CREATED); call CreateTriggerIngressEndpoint first. Also returned when the connection does not exist in this environment (RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND).J\325\001\n\003409\022\315\001\n\312\001A rotation is already in progress - confirm the current one with ConfirmTriggerSigningSecretRotation before generating another, or the secret the customer has just installed would be retired unconfirmed\202\265\030\034\n\030connected_accounts:write\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002P\"K/api/v1/connections/{connection_id}/trigger-ingress/signing-secret/generate:\001*'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['SyncTriggerDefinitions']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['SyncTriggerDefinitions']._serialized_options = b'\222A\377\013\n\010Triggers\022)Replace a connector\'s trigger definitions\032\220\007Replaces every trigger definition one connector declares, together with its provider identity path when supplied. Synced from the connector repository on every merge, the same mechanism that already syncs that repository\'s tool and provider definitions. The call is a full replace: a definition previously stored for this provider but absent from this request is removed, so a rename or a deletion in the connector repository is reflected exactly rather than surviving under its old name. An empty definitions list clears the provider\'s entire trigger catalogue. A definition marked draft is accepted but not stored, and is named in skipped_drafts, since a draft has not been confirmed against its provider and must not become live through a sync. Restricted to Scalekit\'s own internal client - trigger definitions are catalogue-global, shared by every tenant, so no customer-facing credential may write to them.J\360\001\n\003200\022\350\001\n\253\001The provider\'s trigger catalogue was replaced; the response reports how many definitions were stored, which were skipped as drafts, and whether an identity path was stored\0228\n6\0324.scalekit.v1.triggers.SyncTriggerDefinitionsResponseJt\n\003400\022m\nkA definition failed validation, or a definition\'s own provider field does not match the provider in the URLJ9\n\003401\0222\n0The caller is not Scalekit\'s own internal clientJ\220\001\n\003409\022\210\001\n\205\001A definition would change an already-published trigger version, which is immutable - ship the change as a new trigger_version instead\202\265\030\002\030\004\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002+\032&/api/v1/trigger-definitions/{provider}:\001*'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['ListTriggerDeliveryAttemptsByEndpoint']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['ListTriggerDeliveryAttemptsByEndpoint']._serialized_options = b'\222A\375\005\n\010Triggers\022.List trigger delivery attempts for an endpoint\032\305\003Retrieves the most recent trigger delivery attempts made to one delivery endpoint, newest first. Use this to check whether fired triggers actually reached a customer-configured endpoint and to inspect the response each attempt received. Delivery attempts are not stored in Scalekit\'s own database - this reads directly from the trigger delivery application, and only the most recent attempts per endpoint are returned, with no cross-endpoint pagination.J\221\001\n\003200\022\211\001\n>Successfully retrieved the endpoint\'s recent delivery attempts\022G\nE\032C.scalekit.v1.triggers.ListTriggerDeliveryAttemptsByEndpointResponseJe\n\003400\022^\n\\endpoint_id is missing or does not belong to this environment\'s trigger delivery application\202\265\030\021\n\rwebhooks:read\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002;\0229/api/v1/trigger-delivery/endpoints/{endpoint_id}/attempts'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['ListTriggerDeliveriesByEndpoint']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['ListTriggerDeliveriesByEndpoint']._serialized_options = b'\222A\352\014\n\010Triggers\022&List trigger deliveries to an endpoint\032\241\007Retrieves one page of the trigger events delivered to one delivery endpoint, newest first, one entry per event however many attempts it took. Use this to check whether fired triggers reached a customer-configured endpoint, and to find the deliveries that failed or are still being retried. Narrow the list by delivery state (`status`), by the connection or trigger subscription the event came from (`channel`), and by time (`after`, `before`). Each delivery names the trigger, its detection mode and the connection it came from when they still exist. Pass `next_page_token` back as `page_token`, with the same endpoint and filters, to read older deliveries. Read the attempts behind one delivery with ListTriggerDeliveryAttemptsByMessage. Deliveries are not stored in Scalekit\'s own database: this reads directly from the trigger delivery application, which keeps them for 90 days. The event payload is never fetched or returned.J\313\001\n\003200\022\303\001\n~Successfully retrieved one page of the endpoint\'s deliveries, newest first, with a next_page_token when older deliveries exist\022A\n?\032=.scalekit.v1.triggers.ListTriggerDeliveriesByEndpointResponseJ\262\002\n\003400\022\252\002\n\247\002endpoint_id is missing or does not belong to this environment\'s trigger delivery application; page_token is malformed or was issued for a different environment, endpoint or set of filters; channel is not a connection or trigger subscription in this environment; or `after` is later than `before`J\216\001\n\003429\022\206\001\n\203\001The trigger delivery application is refusing reads for volume. Try again shortly; the request is not retried on the caller\'s behalf\202\265\030\021\n\rwebhooks:read\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002=\022;/api/v1/trigger-delivery/endpoints/{endpoint_id}/deliveries'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['ListTriggerDeliveryAttemptsByMessage']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['ListTriggerDeliveryAttemptsByMessage']._serialized_options = b'\222A\353\010\n\010Triggers\022%List the attempts behind one delivery\032\232\004Retrieves every attempt made to deliver one trigger event to one delivery endpoint, oldest first: the first attempt, each automatic retry, and any manual retry. Use this to show how one delivery from ListTriggerDeliveriesByEndpoint went, attempt by attempt. Each attempt carries its outcome, HTTP status code, response body, response time and the URL it was sent to, and whether it was a scheduled attempt or a manual retry. Returns at most 50 attempts; `truncated` is true when the delivery has more. The event payload is never returned.J\272\001\n\003200\022\262\001\nhSuccessfully retrieved the delivery\'s attempts, oldest first, with truncated set when more than 50 exist\022F\nD\032B.scalekit.v1.triggers.ListTriggerDeliveryAttemptsByMessageResponseJ\314\001\n\003400\022\304\001\n\301\001endpoint_id is missing, msg_id is not a message ID (msg_ followed by 1 to 64 letters or digits), or the endpoint or the message does not exist in this environment\'s trigger delivery applicationJ\216\001\n\003429\022\206\001\n\203\001The trigger delivery application is refusing reads for volume. Try again shortly; the request is not retried on the caller\'s behalf\202\265\030\021\n\rwebhooks:read\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002O\022M/api/v1/trigger-delivery/endpoints/{endpoint_id}/deliveries/{msg_id}/attempts'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['LookupTriggerDelivery']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['LookupTriggerDelivery']._serialized_options = b'\222A\331\013\n\010Triggers\022\035Find a trigger delivery by ID\032\372\005Finds one trigger event by its message ID or its event ID, and returns every delivery attempt made for it across every delivery endpoint in this environment, oldest first. Use this when a developer holds an ID from their own server: the message ID arrives as the `webhook-id` header, and the event ID is the payload\'s `dedupe_key`. Attempt IDs are not accepted. Only this environment\'s trigger delivery application is searched, so an ID from another environment is reported as not found. The response names the trigger and the connection the event came from when they still exist, and each attempt names the endpoint and URL it was sent to. Returns at most 50 attempts; `truncated` is true when the event has more. The event payload is never fetched or returned.J\354\001\n\003200\022\344\001\n\250\001Found the event; includes its message ID, event ID and creation time, the trigger and connection it came from, and its delivery attempts to every endpoint, oldest first\0227\n5\0323.scalekit.v1.triggers.LookupTriggerDeliveryResponseJ\272\001\n\003400\022\262\001\n\257\001id is empty, longer than 256 characters, contains characters outside letters, digits, `.`, `_` and `-`, is an attempt ID (`atmpt_`), or is neither a message ID nor an event IDJt\n\003404\022m\nkNo trigger event with this message ID or event ID exists in this environment\'s trigger delivery applicationJ\216\001\n\003429\022\206\001\n\203\001The trigger delivery application is refusing reads for volume. Try again shortly; the request is not retried on the caller\'s behalf\202\265\030\021\n\rwebhooks:read\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002*\022(/api/v1/trigger-delivery/deliveries/{id}'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['BatchLookupTriggerDeliveries']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['BatchLookupTriggerDeliveries']._serialized_options = b'\222A\330\020\n\010Triggers\022%Find several trigger deliveries by ID\032\336\007Finds up to 25 trigger events by message ID or event ID, and returns each one\'s delivery attempts across every delivery endpoint in this environment, as LookupTriggerDelivery does for one. Use this when a developer holds a list of IDs from their own server, for example to check which of a day\'s events reached them. Each ID gets its own result, in the order sent: FOUND with the delivery, NOT_FOUND, RATE_LIMITED, FAILED, or NOT_ATTEMPTED. Attempts in a batch result carry no response body; look one delivery up with LookupTriggerDelivery to read the bodies. Only this environment\'s trigger delivery application is searched, so an ID from another environment comes back as NOT_FOUND. When the trigger delivery application refuses a read for volume, that ID comes back RATE_LIMITED and the batch stops: IDs not yet looked up come back NOT_ATTEMPTED, to be sent again shortly. The call only reads, so retrying the whole request is always safe. The event payload is never fetched or returned.J\300\001\n\003200\022\270\001\nvRequest processed; every ID has its own result in results, in the order sent, and found_count says how many were found\022>\n<\032:.scalekit.v1.triggers.BatchLookupTriggerDeliveriesResponseJ\274\003\n\003400\022\264\003\n\261\003The request was refused as a whole: `ids` is empty, holds more than 25 IDs (`at most 25 ids per request`) or repeats one; an ID is longer than 256 characters or contains characters outside letters, digits, `.`, `_` and `-`; or an ID is an attempt ID (`atmpt_`), which LookupTriggerDelivery refuses too. A well-formed ID that names no trigger event in this environment is NOT this case: it comes back as a NOT_FOUND result with a 200.J\254\001\n\003404\022\244\001\n\241\001AgentKit Triggers is not enabled for this workspace. Refused as a whole. An ID that names no trigger event is NOT this case: it is a NOT_FOUND result with a 200.J\222\002\n\003429\022\212\002\n\207\002This environment has sent too many batch lookup requests in the last minute. Refused as a whole; retry after a short wait. The trigger delivery application refusing reads partway through a batch is NOT this case: it is reported per ID as RATE_LIMITED, with a 200.\202\265\030\021\n\rwebhooks:read\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\0024\"//api/v1/trigger-delivery/deliveries:batchLookup:\001*'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['BatchResendTriggerDeliveries']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['BatchResendTriggerDeliveries']._serialized_options = b'\222A\307\025\n\010Triggers\022!Resend several trigger deliveries\032\236\nQueues a new delivery attempt for up to 50 trigger events, each to one delivery endpoint, and reports what happened to each target separately. Use this to retry deliveries that failed, for example a selection from ListTriggerDeliveriesByEndpoint, instead of resending them one at a time. Each target names a message ID and the endpoint to deliver that event to again. QUEUED means the trigger delivery application accepted a new manual attempt, not that the event was delivered: the attempt and its outcome appear in ListTriggerDeliveryAttemptsByMessage as a TRIGGER_DELIVERY_ATTEMPT_TRIGGER_TYPE_MANUAL attempt. The call is best-effort: a target that fails does not undo one that was queued. Resending is not idempotent: each time a target is sent it queues one more attempt, so after a partial failure retry only the targets that came back RATE_LIMITED, FAILED or NOT_ATTEMPTED, never the whole request. A FAILED target whose request timed out may still have been queued, so retrying it can queue a second attempt. A FAILED target whose event payload is no longer stored can never be resent; its reason says so. When a target is rate limited the batch stops: targets not yet started come back NOT_ATTEMPTED, while a target already in flight finishes with its own outcome. The event payload is never returned.J\331\001\n\003200\022\321\001\n\216\001Request processed; every target has its own result in results, in the order sent, and queued_count says how many new attempts this call queued\022>\n<\032:.scalekit.v1.triggers.BatchResendTriggerDeliveriesResponseJ\275\005\n\003400\022\265\005\n\262\005The request was refused as a whole and nothing was queued: `targets` is empty or holds more than 50 (`at most 50 targets per request`); a `msg_id` is not a message ID (`msg_` followed by 1 to 64 letters or digits) or an `endpoint_id` is not an endpoint ID (`ep_` followed by 1 to 64 letters or digits); or the same `msg_id` and `endpoint_id` pair appears twice (`duplicate target at index N: each msg_id and endpoint_id pair may appear only once`, N counting from 0), since sending it twice would queue two attempts. A well-formed message or endpoint ID that does not exist in this environment\'s trigger delivery application is NOT this case: it comes back as a NOT_FOUND result with a 200.J\310\001\n\003404\022\300\001\n\275\001AgentKit Triggers is not enabled for this workspace. Refused as a whole; nothing was queued. A message or endpoint that does not exist is NOT this case: it is a NOT_FOUND result with a 200.J\217\002\n\003429\022\207\002\n\204\002This environment has sent too many batch resend requests in the last minute. Refused as a whole; nothing was queued. Retry after a short wait. A rate limit reached partway through a batch is NOT this case: it is reported per target as RATE_LIMITED, with a 200.\202\265\030\022\n\016webhooks:write\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\0024\"//api/v1/trigger-delivery/deliveries:batchResend:\001*'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['RecoverTriggerDeliveries']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['RecoverTriggerDeliveries']._serialized_options = b'\222A\304\032\n\010Triggers\022\'Resend failed deliveries to an endpoint\032\231\013Starts a recovery that resends every trigger event whose delivery to one endpoint failed within a time window. Use this once an endpoint is healthy again after an outage, instead of resending each failed delivery with BatchResendTriggerDeliveries. The recovery covers events with a failed attempt to this endpoint from `since` up to, not including, `until`. It skips an event that also has a successful attempt to this endpoint within the window, and it never sends an event that was not already sent to this endpoint. The window is at most 14 days; omit `until` to recover up to the present. The recovery runs asynchronously in the trigger delivery application after this call returns, so the response confirms that it started, not that anything was delivered. Each recovered event gets a new attempt, which appears in ListTriggerDeliveriesByEndpoint and ListTriggerDeliveryAttemptsByMessage like any other. One call recovers at most 10,000 events and stops there without reporting it: when more may have failed, cover the period with several calls over narrower windows. Recovery progress cannot be read through the API. `task_id` identifies this recovery in Scalekit\'s logs for support, and there is no endpoint to poll it. Not idempotent: calling again re-queues every event that is still failed, and a second call made before the first finishes can queue a second attempt for the same event. The event payload is never returned.J\345\002\n\003200\022\335\002\n\236\002The recovery started; includes its task_id and its status when this call returned. Events are resent asynchronously after this response. In the rare case that the trigger delivery application accepted the recovery without naming it, task_id and status are empty; the recovery still runs\022:\n8\0326.scalekit.v1.triggers.RecoverTriggerDeliveriesResponseJ\213\005\n\003400\022\203\005\n\200\005The request was refused as a whole and no recovery started: `endpoint_id` is not an endpoint ID (`ep_` followed by 1 to 64 letters or digits); `since` is missing (`since is required`), or `since` or `until` is not a valid timestamp; or the window is invalid \342\200\224 `since` is in the future (`since must not be in the future`), `until` is in the future (`until must not be in the future; omit it to recover up to now`), `until` is not after `since` (`until must be after since`), or `until` is more than 14 days after `since`, measured to now when `until` is omitted (`the recovery window must be at most 14 days; split it into several calls`).J\247\001\n\003404\022\237\001\n\234\001The endpoint does not exist in this environment\'s trigger delivery application, or AgentKit Triggers is not enabled for this workspace. No recovery started.J\302\001\n\003429\022\272\001\n\267\001This environment has started too many recoveries in a short period, or the trigger delivery application is refusing requests for volume. No recovery started; retry after a short wait.J\215\004\n\003500\022\205\004\n\202\004The recovery was not confirmed. Either the trigger delivery application refused it (`could not start the recovery`), or the request timed out or failed after it may have reached that application (`could not confirm the recovery started; it may be running \342\200\224 check the endpoint\'s deliveries before retrying`). In the second case the recovery may be running: look for new manual attempts with ListTriggerDeliveriesByEndpoint before calling again, because a second call can queue a second attempt for the same event.\202\265\030\022\n\016webhooks:write\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002=\"8/api/v1/trigger-delivery/endpoints/{endpoint_id}:recover:\001*'
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['TriggerWebhookWrapper']._loaded_options = None
  _globals['_TRIGGERSERVICE'].methods_by_name['TriggerWebhookWrapper']._serialized_options = b'\222A\262\016\n\010Triggers\0223Proxy a request to the trigger delivery application\032\251\010Forwards a request to the application that delivers this environment\'s fired trigger events, scoped to that application only - never the environment\'s general webhook application. Used to list, create, update and delete the endpoints that receive trigger deliveries, and to read their delivery attempts, through the provider\'s own REST shape below /api/v1/trigger-delivery/wb/. Provisions the trigger delivery application on first use, if one does not already exist. Message reads never return event payloads: a GET of `msg`, `msg/{msg_id}` or `endpoint/{endpoint_id}/msg` is always forwarded with `with_content=false`, replacing any `with_content` the request sent, so those responses carry each message\'s metadata without its payload. Governed by the webhooks permissions, not the connected-accounts ones: reading endpoints, messages and delivery attempts requires webhooks:read, while reading or rotating an endpoint\'s signing secret, resending a delivery, creating, updating or deleting an endpoint, and any path not recognised as a read require webhooks:write.JX\n\003200\022Q\nOThe proxied request succeeded; the response body is the provider\'s own responseJ\352\004\n\003400\022\342\004\n\337\004The request was refused before anything was forwarded or provisioned, for any HTTP method: the path does not begin with exactly /api/v1/trigger-delivery/wb/ (a percent-encoded or re-cased prefix is refused); a segment below that prefix percent-encodes a slash (`%2F`); the path below the prefix is empty, or contains an empty, `.` or `..` segment (encoded forms such as `%2E%2E` included), a control character or invalid percent-encoding; or a GET of `msg`, `msg/{msg_id}` or `endpoint/{endpoint_id}/msg` has a query string that cannot be parsed, such as one with invalid percent-encoding or a `;` separator\202\265\030\021\n\rwebhooks:read\030D\372\322\344\223\002\t\022\007PREVIEW\202\323\344\223\002\322\001\022\036/api/v1/trigger-delivery/wb/**Z.\"\036/api/v1/trigger-delivery/wb/**:\014request_bodyZ.\032\036/api/v1/trigger-delivery/wb/**:\014request_bodyZ.2\036/api/v1/trigger-delivery/wb/**:\014request_bodyZ *\036/api/v1/trigger-delivery/wb/**'
